// Sub-plan F fixture: what is live where a function ends.
//
// An exit block has no successors, so backward liveness needs a seed there,
// and the seed is **everything** — every register, every flag, on every
// function, with no narrowing (`plans/05-optimizer.md` decision 12). Without
// one, the seed is the empty set and the optimizer deletes return values. The
// symptom is a wrong program rather than a compiler error, which is why this
// fixture lands with the block view rather than with the pass that first makes
// the need visible.
//
// **F wrote three reasons here and all three were about how a call is
// spelled**: `call foo` opts out of contract checking, a hand-written
// `jsr foo` never had a contract, and an undeclared clobber is `K816-W0123` —
// a warning rather than a guarantee. Sub-plan G's decision 19 closed them.
// A clause can name a flag now — `-> a, c` is what `carry` below would need —
// and an output a clause does not list is undefined after the call at *every*
// call site, `call foo` included. The escape hatch escapes the check, not the
// rule: a caller may still write the read, and may not rely on it.
//
// What stops the narrowing is elsewhere, and G13 demonstrated it: a tail
// `goto` lets control enter a section without calling it, and that section's
// `rts` returns to the jumping function's caller — bound by a different
// clause. Its own clause is then the wrong contract for its return.
//
// G13 also priced the deferral: three instructions and six bytes over
// `examples/`, the goldens and the imported corpus, one of them the `ldy` in
// `produce` below. Four of 244 emitted sections declare an output at all.
// `tests/live_outs.rs` holds both measurements; `postmortem.md` §G.22 argues
// them.
//
// `caller` earns `K816-W0123` on purpose: it reads Y after a checked call that
// writes it, and calls `produce` both ways so the two spellings sit side by
// side. Since decision 19 that warning is a warning about *undefined* state —
// which is why the file's own `ldy` is one of the three instructions G13
// measures, and why nothing here is deleted anyway: the seed is everything.

var CELL = $0400

// `produce` declares `-> a`. The final `lda` survives because it is the
// declared output; the final `ldy` survives **too**, because the seed is
// everything — not because reading Y after this call is defined. It is not
// (decision 19), which is why G13 measures this `ldy` as one of the three
// instructions a narrowing rule would delete.
func produce @a8 @i8 (a) -> a {
    a+#$01
    ldy #$09
}

// The trailing `clc` survives. A clause could declare the carry now — `-> c`
// is legal since sub-plan G — and this one does not, so the seed keeps it:
// callers set and read the carry as an answer, and no diagnostic told them
// not to.
func carry @a8 @i8 {
    lda #$01
    CELL = a
    clc
}

// The same function called both ways: through the checked form, which states
// the flow, and through the escape hatch, which does not. Both lower to the
// same `jsr`, which is why the optimizer cannot tell them apart afterwards —
// and why it treats every callee's exit the same way.
func caller @a8 @i8 {
    a = #$01
    produce a -> a
    CELL = a
    CELL,y = a

    a = #$02
    call produce
    CELL = a
    CELL,y = a
}

// Mid-function, the identical load goes: `lda #$07` writes A, N, and Z, and
// `lda #$08` overwrites all three before anything reads them. The difference
// from `produce` is only *where* it sits, which is exactly the claim.
func middle @a8 @i8 {
    lda #$07
    lda #$08
    CELL = a
}
